WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.011
"Saffron for Treating Age-Related
Macular Degeneration"

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.160
In addition to two
to three daily servings

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.920
of green leafy vegetables,

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.160
berries are considered
a healthy choice

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:16.240
for the preservation
of eye health.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.920
As we age, our critical
retinal pigment epithelium layer

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.560
starts to break down.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.240
However, we may be able
to decelerate that aging

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.920
with blueberries.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.360
Human RPE cells bathed in
blueberry anthocyanins

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.240
had fewer free radicals

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.080
and a lower proportion
of aged cells,

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:35.200
suggesting that blueberries
and other red, blue, and purple

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.680
pigmented fruits and vegetables

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:40.560
may help prevent age-related
macular degeneration.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.960
Protective properties
have also been demonstrated

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.520
in vitro on RPE cells

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.280
from purple
sweet potato anthocyanin

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.920
as well as the yellow pigment
in turmeric and an amla extract

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.800
(Indian gooseberry).

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.160
However, the only
human interventional trials

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.200
on berries for vision
were a month-long trial

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.480
of a bilberry extract
that demonstrated

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.320
significantly less eye strain

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.960
after iPad use
compared to placebo

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.280
and a study showing
three weeks of blueberry juice

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.240
or freeze-dried blueberry powder

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.000
both significantly improved
photostress recovery time,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.080
which is the time needed
to recuperate visual acuity

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:18.720
after bright light exposure

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.600
by about five seconds.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.040
Only one pigmented food
has been put to the test

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.120
in macular
degeneration patients:

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.040
the spice saffron.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.560
Saffron is so potent
that the whole spice

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.920
can be stuffed into capsules
to pit them head-to-head

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.560
against placebo.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.680
Those with early-stage
age-related macular degeneration

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.040
were randomized to a tiny pinch
(20 mg, about 8 threads)

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.480
of saffron versus placebo
for three months.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.160
Compared to the placebo group,

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.400
the saffron group experienced
a significant improvement

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.880
in retinal flicker sensitivity--

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.120
that’s our ability to pick out

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:53.880
high frequency light pulses

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.320
(rather than seeing
the flickering light

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:56.840
as a steady beam)

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.680
that diminishes with age.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.320
It’s a sensitive test
of retinal function,

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.800
but it is of questionable
clinical value.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.360
However, there was
a small improvement

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:08.680
in visual acuity in 80 percent
of the saffron subjects

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.880
(being able to see one line
further down on an eye chart)

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.280
versus none improving
in the placebo group.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.200
This is particularly important

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.400
for those with age-related

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.600
macular degeneration

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.600
for whom vision tends
to get worse over time,

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.480
not better.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.280
What about a more
substantial pinch?

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.280
(50 mg, about 20 threads)

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.280
Compared to placebo,
three months of this larger dose

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.660
led to a similar
improvement in visual acuity

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.600
in macular degeneration
patients, suggesting more

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.520
is not necessarily better.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.040
Saffron is the most
expensive spice there is,

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.160
selling for about $200
an ounce (28 g),

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.800
so being able to get away
with a 20 mg dose

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.560
(about 15 cents a day)

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.760
rather than a 50 mg dose
(closer to 40 cents a day)

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.280
could add up over the long run.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.320
Even smaller doses may work too,

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.680
but they just
haven’t been tested.

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:58.680
What about
the tiny pinch over a year

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.240
instead of just three months?

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.360
Early-stage macular patients
were given 20 mg of saffron

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.480
a day for an average
of 14 months

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.000
and average visual acuity

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.920
improved by two eye chart lines.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:11.920
They also reported
an improvement in contrast

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.080
and color perception,
reading ability,

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:15.000
low-light vision,

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.400
and overall quality of life.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.800
The problem is the study
lacked a control group,

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.440
so it’s hard to put much stock

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.400
in subjective measures
of improvement.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.960
Interestingly, a six-month
randomized, double-blind,

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.280
placebo-controlled trial

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:30.960
found no objective
acuity improvement

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.560
in either the placebo group

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.360
or a 30 mg a day saffron group,

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.560
yet reported that the patients

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.640
unwittingly in the saffron group

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:42.240
gave subjective reports
of better vision

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.280
and general wellbeing.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.520
The researchers suggest

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.640
this may be an
antidepressant effect,

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.160
and indeed a meta-analysis
of a dozen

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.680
randomized, double-blind,
placebo-controlled trials

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.720
of saffron for depression
found it beat out placebo

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:58.880
and was found comparable
to antidepressant drugs.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.440
The largest saffron
vision trial to date

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.360
was a double-blind,
placebo-controlled

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.760
crossover trial
in which 100 older adults

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.640
with mild or moderate

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.600
age-related macular degeneration

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.960
were randomized to 20 mg
of saffron a day

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.560
or placebo for three months,

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.160
then switched
to the opposite group

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.000
for an additional three months.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.720
When on the tiny
daily pinch of saffron

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.440
there was a significant,
yet modest improvement

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:23.800
in visual acuity,

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:26.680
and this was the case
for both those on and off

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:28.520
best practice therapy

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.440
(the beta-carotene free
AREDS supplementation),

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:50.320
suggesting an additive benefit.

